# Prove session destruction and temporary cleanup

## Scope and existing evidence

Close, refresh, cancel, crash, and restart fixtures must show that ephemeral
state and media intermediates disappear while explicit exports remain intact.

Current runtime evidence proves orderly process termination releases the old
loopback listener, restart generates a fresh session credential, and a stale
credential cannot authenticate to the new process. A colocated runtime fixture
now drops its owner and immediately rebinds the exact OS-assigned loopback
endpoint, pinning listener release directly.

The secret adapter separately pins that independent operating-system-backed
generations do not reuse the same encoded credential. Process-level draft
fixtures
also write and read task, source, and raw-response text, then verify both
orderly restart and forced process death yield empty fields in the fresh
session. A checked-in application-process fixture now also populates an accepted
semantic revision containing two semantic asset records and a figure reference,
retains distinct raw byte sequences for both assets, commits text and
asset-reference history, and leaves one terminal media job with a registered
waveform plus retry-required cleanup bookkeeping in process memory. Orderly exit
and forced process
death both leave a fresh owner with no accepted revision, history position, raw
bytes, or prior media-job authority.

A 128-job interleaving regression additionally verifies that independent
process-local media jobs preserve their own cleanup requirements, failed
cleanup retries, and settled states even when supplied with another job's
waveform identity. This is in-memory bookkeeping only, not actual file cleanup.

Media decoding and actual temporary
conversion-file cleanup remain open because no filesystem intermediate exists
yet.

While private draft text is live, the runtime changes no declared repository
runtime-root file. The fuller application-process fixture also proves on Linux
that populated draft, semantic history, retained asset bytes, derived results,
and retry-required media cleanup add no writable regular-file descriptors beyond
process descriptors inherited from the test harness.

Checked-in browser policy tests guard one-time launch-credential fragment
consumption, credential invalidation on `pagehide`, in-flight handshake and
draft request cancellation, pending clipboard/draft invalidation, session-text
clearing, bfcache subtree scrubbing, and mandatory reload when a persisted page
is restored. A `401`
during handshake, draft
hydration, or draft replacement now invalidates the same page-session work and
scrubs all four session text surfaces before requiring restart.

Aborted draft requests and stale `413` diagnostic-body completions cannot
repopulate status text after invalidation. Concurrent field failures also remain
visible until the failed field succeeds; another field cannot announce
`Session ready` while work or failure remains. A real Firefox coalescing fixture
now holds one task replacement, edits the field again, returns a valid `413` for
the obsolete value, and requires the newer complete value to be posted next and
restore `Session ready`.

A separate real Firefox fixture hydrates all three draft fields through a
deterministic same-origin session,
confirms the launch fragment has been scrubbed, reloads that exact tab, and
requires the refreshed document to expose blank disabled draft fields without
issuing another handshake or draft request. Refresh therefore cannot recover the
launch credential from the rewritten browser URL or persistence API.

A second real Firefox path hydrates the page, holds an authenticated task
replacement in flight, closes that exact browsing context, and observes the
request transport abort while Firefox remains alive. This covers browser-close
cancellation of page-session draft work, not cancellation of backend application
operations.

Handshake response admission now invalidates `401` directly from the status and
parses JSON bodies only for the two body-bearing handshake result classes,
`200` and `409`; unrelated statuses cannot delay failure on an irrelevant body.
The localhost adapter also JSON-escapes compatible and required backend version
identities, so application-owned quotes, backslashes, controls, and Unicode
cannot corrupt the handshake response syntax.

An explicit, confirmation-gated **End session** control now sends an
exact-origin Bearer-authenticated, empty-body POST while the page remains
active. The backend rejects missing or incorrect origin/credential and
nonempty bodies; on acceptance it acknowledges closure, leaves its loopback
accept loop, releases the listener, and drops the process application owner.

A red-first browser regression found that a draft replacement completing
after the user armed End session, or after an End request failed, could
replace the warning with `Session ready`. The browser now preserves the
confirmation warning while armed and retains the failed-erasure warning
until the session is actually invalidated. The same protection covers a
late rejected draft write, without suppressing that field's retry state.

Extracted-adapter tests exercise all three orderings; a real Firefox
fixture holds a draft POST, returns `503` for End, then acknowledges the
pending draft and verifies the failed-end message remains visible. This
protects UI state reporting, not a claim that a failed End erased data.

A second red-first regression found that a rejected draft POST could hide the
explicit **End session** confirmation warning while it was armed. The browser
now keeps the destruction warning visible through a late draft failure; when
confirmation is dismissed, it reveals that failure and retains the edit retry.
A later successful synchronization instead restores `Session ready` only after
all draft writes settle.

An extracted-adapter test and a real Firefox fixture both reproduce the prior
incorrect warning and verify the corrected order. This is UI confirmation
integrity, not backend erasure evidence.

Unlike handshake and draft requests, the confirmed End fetch previously
omitted the page-lifetime abort signal. A red-first source contract now requires
that signal alongside the per-request abort and six-second deadline.

A real Firefox fixture withholds End response headers, then dispatches
`pagehide` without navigation and observes socket closure and private draft
scrubbing. This proves client teardown without relying on browser navigation
behavior; it does not prove backend erasure or rollback.

A real socket regression verifies that an unauthorized attempt cannot end the
session and that an authorized request releases the exact endpoint. Firefox
coverage verifies confirmation before any mutation, authenticated termination,
and immediate local draft erasure. Authenticated browser POST requests use an
origin-only referrer policy so Firefox supplies the canonical Origin header
without revealing a URL path or fragment.

GET hydration still suppresses the referrer. Browser `pagehide` continues
to wipe local state independently: network teardown is not promised for tabs
that close without the explicit command, or for crashed or unresponsive
processes.

Browser draft hydration now admits an HTTP 200 body only when its response
declares the expected `text/plain; charset=utf-8` media type. A red-first
runtime-extracted browser regression showed a 200 HTML response previously
became accepted draft text. The corrected path refuses wrong or absent media
without reading the response body.

Hydration leaves all three inputs disabled
until every field passes and can never apply a partial snapshot. The independent
browser transport fixture also pins case-insensitive exact media admission.

Firefox also verifies that an incomplete HTTP `409` handshake exposes a
same-tab retry without draft admission, while a subsequently valid, typed
`409` version mismatch remains terminal. Neither response restores the
scrubbed fragment, enables draft editing, or reads private fields. The
terminal state remains inert even when a synthetic click event targets its
hidden retry control.

A real Firefox loopback fixture now returns an HTML media type with a
successful status for the source draft after the task draft has arrived. It
verifies the complete three-field snapshot stays blank and disabled, the
candidate field is not requested, and a later valid-media retry hydrates all
fields. This covers browser network and DOM behavior beyond the isolated parser
fixture.

The end confirmation can be cancelled by Escape or by moving focus away from
its button, preventing a stale armed action. An authenticated end returning
`401` now invalidates browser authority and clears private draft fields. A
transient `503` retains the draft and resets confirmation so the user can
retry without falsely announcing successful destruction. The Firefox suite
asserts both outcomes and a successful subsequent retry, in addition to
confirmation cancellation and cleanup.

A Linux process fixture now completes an explicitly selected profile Export,
forcibly kills its process after the synced file effect, and verifies that the
exported ZIP remains readable through the independent secure selected-profile
Import path in a fresh process owner. The ZIP includes a syntax-valid JSON
section and opaque binary asset, both independently SHA-verified after process
death.

The same process-death fixture now covers authorized replacement of a distinct,
previously valid profile ZIP as well as preserve-existing creation. After
SIGKILL, a fresh selected-profile Import independently verifies both replacement
members and their archive digest, with no staging name or prior process receipt.

The new owner has no inherited Export
retry receipt and cannot replay the same key over a preserve-existing target;
no Export staging file remains after the successful commit. The readiness
marker is test-only and removed before checking surviving directory entries.

A separate low-level Linux file-commit fixture now kills a process while
writing an incomplete profile through anonymous `O_TMPFILE` staging. Both
preserve-existing and replacement dispositions leave no named partial stage;
the original replacement target survives unchanged. This is not a proof of
cleanup if the process dies in the brief replacement link-before-rename window.
The test-owned readiness marker is removed after each forced kill.

The same Linux child-process harness now also suspends the final cancellation
observation **after** the full anonymous file has been synced but **before**
its first link, then kills the child with SIGKILL. Both preserve-existing and
replacement runs prove no staged filename escapes and the replacement target
remains unchanged. The harness verifies actual SIGKILL termination, not only
an unsuccessful exit. This extends the precommit crash proof to synced bytes;
it still does not cover a crash in the later link-before-rename window.

A separate Linux process fixture now blocks only after an authorized
replacement returns a fully synced file-commit result. The parent verifies
SIGKILL termination, then reads the exact complete 131,073-byte replacement
under owner-only `0600` permissions, with no random staging name left behind.
Both an originally present target and an absent target are exercised. This
proves survival after successful replacement, not durability at the earlier
link-before-rename crash point where an orphaned stage may still survive.

A red-first browser adapter regression found that an already-rejected
handshake, draft GET, or draft POST response could retain an unread body while
editing remained disabled or a retry was offered. The browser now explicitly
cancels rejected response bodies and aborts the individual in-flight fetch,
while preserving the page-lifetime cancellation signal on handshake and draft
operations.

This includes stale responses, authorization failures, unexpected
statuses, and wrong-media responses; the explicit End session command also
cancels unread refusal bodies. Three executable browser-module fixtures verify
refused streams are cancelled without consuming private payloads or changing
retry and diagnostic outcomes.

A separate Firefox test serves a wrong-media handshake with a body that never
ends: the per-request abort closes that live connection, leaves all inputs
disabled, and permits the same fragment-free tab to retry, hydrate all three
fields, and enable editing. Firefox additionally
showed that body-stream cancellation alone did not promptly close this socket,
which is why both cancellation and request abort are used.

This is page-owned browser transport cleanup evidence, not proof that closing
a tab terminates the backend process or cleans up media conversions.

The task remains open until temporary media exist and end-to-end fixtures also
cover backend operation cancellation, media cleanup, and late-link crash
recovery rather than only anonymous-stage and post-sync termination.

Browser draft hydration now refuses malformed UTF-8 instead of displaying a
lossily decoded partial snapshot. A Firefox loopback fixture serves invalid
source bytes after a valid task read: all three draft inputs remain blank and
disabled, and the candidate read is never sent. An explicit retry with a valid
leading U+FEFF source body restores all fields with that character intact.
This tests browser decoding and retry, not backend media-file cleanup.

A red-first browser adapter fixture found that an otherwise valid UTF-8 draft
GET could hydrate more than the backend's 1,048,576-byte per-field ceiling.
Browser draft reads now enforce that same limit from declared response length
and from actual streamed bytes, cancelling an overlong body without first
buffering its entire response. The incremental fatal UTF-8 decoder preserves a
leading U+FEFF and code points split across network chunks; interrupted reads
remain unavailable rather than becoming accepted partial text.

Focused tests
cover exact-limit admission, over-limit rejection, early declared-size refusal,
malformed UTF-8, and transport failure. A real Firefox fixture now refuses
both corrupt and oversized source responses without hydrating any of the three
fields, then accepts a valid retry. This is a browser draft ingress bound, not
proof of backend media conversion or temporary-file cleanup.

The shared browser response reader now also stops on the sixty-fifth
zero-length stream chunk, even when the byte total remains under the draft
limit. A red-first stream fixture verified that sixty-five empty chunks
followed by valid draft text were previously admitted; a bounded read now
cancels without hydrating. Sixty-four empty chunks followed by valid UTF-8
remain accepted. This is client-side liveness protection, not media cleanup.

A further red-first browser regression demonstrated that a correctly typed
handshake JSON or draft body can deliver a short prefix and never close, below
both existing byte and empty-chunk limits. All four session fetches now carry
a six-second browser-side request deadline, active through response-body reads
as well as response headers. It complements rather than replaces the runtime's
two-second I/O budget. A response that exceeds the deadline cannot admit a
partial handshake or draft snapshot, and an explicit End session timeout never
claims successful erasure.

A real Firefox loopback fixture keeps a valid-media handshake, draft GET, and
`413` draft diagnostic body open after sending an incomplete prefix. Firefox
closes each connection at its browser request deadline. The first two failures
retain blank, disabled draft inputs and recover through same-tab connection
retries; the stalled diagnostic preserves the unsent edit and exposes an edit
retry, which succeeds once the fixture acknowledges it.

Independent Node fixtures exercise all three stalled readers and pin deadline
coverage for the explicit End session fetch. This protects browser request
lifetime only; the
remaining backend media cancellation and cleanup proof is still open.

A real Firefox explicit-End fixture also withholds all response headers. Its
request deadline aborts the stalled socket without reporting successful
session erasure or clearing the user's editable draft. A fresh confirmed End
in the same tab succeeds and then clears the page's private text. The first
request's backend outcome is unknown, so this does not demonstrate server-side
idempotency or actual media cleanup after an interrupted End operation.

Replacement to an originally absent profile target now requests Linux
`RENAME_NOREPLACE` at the final atomic rename. A separate actor creating that
selected destination after the last metadata check cannot have its new file
overwritten by the Export; `EEXIST` is a proven precommit
`SelectedTargetChanged` refusal; the ordinary error path attempts to remove
the owned random sibling stage. Replacement of an existing, expressly
admitted regular target continues using ordinary atomic rename.

A red-first profile-commit source contract reproduced the missing no-overwrite
flag; an independent real Linux rename fixture verifies the kernel refuses a
late destination while preserving both byte sequences. All 41 file-commit
tests pass, including existing initially absent and present replacement cases.
This does not simulate an injected race *inside* the full Export syscall
sequence. An initially present target may still change after the last check,
and SIGKILL between the temporary link and rename can still strand a stage;
therefore the P1 session-destruction and cleanup task remains open.

An Export cleanup ownership regression showed that a renamed or replaced
temporary sibling could cause the error-path destructor to unlink another
actor's file at the same random name. The Linux file-commit adapter now
records the original staged inode's device and inode, checks the sibling
without following links before failure cleanup, and refuses a changed
sibling before the final rename. Direct test-only fixtures demonstrate the
previous deletion, preservation of substituted regular files and symlinks,
and cleanup of the still-owned sibling. This is a last-observation guard,
not atomic compare-and-unlink: a hostile concurrent rename after the
identity check can still race cleanup or publication, and SIGKILL after
publication of the temporary name can still strand that stage.

Crash-memory capture is a separate privacy boundary. This Linux host
permits unlimited core size and routes crashes to `systemd-coredump` via
`/proc/sys/kernel/core_pattern`; a zero core size limit alone would not
suppress a pipe handler. Atrament now calls Linux `PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0)` through
safe `nix` wrappers at the beginning of `main`, before inspecting private
profile arguments, publishing startup state, or creating a session secret.
`PR_GET_DUMPABLE` verifies the new setting; either system-call failure or an
unexpected dumpable result refuses startup rather than loading private state.

An isolated child-process regression first enables dumpability in that child,
then invokes the production guard and confirms the child's actual Linux
`PR_GET_DUMPABLE` value is false. No deliberate crash, private profile, core
dump, or test-owned persistent artifact is created. This proves the setting
in that test child, not total suppression under every host crash collector or
an end-to-end crash-erasure guarantee. Non-Linux startup, swap, hibernation,
and external browser processes remain outside this Linux-specific guard.

A separate live Linux CLI fixture now launches the actual `atrament`
executable through a disposable fake browser opener and waits until the
runtime publishes `ready`. From the owning parent process, opening its
`/proc/<pid>/maps`, `mem`, and `environ` files is refused with `EACCES` or
`EPERM`. An ordinary sibling child is a positive control: the same parent
can open all three files.

The probe only opens and closes descriptors; it does not read
sensitive memory or environment bytes, create a core dump, or persist
process snapshots. This demonstrates the installed startup guard
on the running binary under this host's procfs policy, beyond the earlier
isolated guard-function test. It cannot prove crash-collector behavior on
other hosts, protect browser-opener argv, or establish complete erasure.

The longer-lived Linux process fixture additionally checks that the
`/proc/<pid>` maps, memory, and environment interfaces remain inaccessible
after an authenticated task draft replacement has been accepted. This checks
the guard while private draft text is resident, not only at startup. It
does not show whether host crash collectors honor the policy.

P1 is still open for broader cancellation, cleanup, and crash coverage.

The live Linux memory probe now also opens, but never reads, `/proc/<pid>/auxv`,
`smaps`, `smaps_rollup`, `pagemap`, and `syscall` from the parent process.
An ordinary same-user child is the positive control for all eight procfs
entries, and both the ready runtime and a runtime holding an accepted private
draft deny every opening with `EACCES` or `EPERM`. These additional kernel
interfaces can disclose process-layout, mapping, page, or register metadata
without reading `/proc/<pid>/mem`. The result is host-specific procfs access
evidence, not an inspection of private bytes, a deliberate crash, a claim about
other operating systems, or proof that host crash collectors erase dumps.

A separate live Linux procfs probe additionally opens the `cwd`, `root`, and
`exe` symlink paths without reading their targets or file contents. An
ordinary same-user child allows all three opens; the real Atrament binary
denies all three with `EACCES` or `EPERM` both when ready and after admitting
private draft text. This checks that the process dumpability guard also
constrains path resolution that could expose process-specific locations. It
does not hide `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`, secure a credential-bearing browser-opener
argument, or prove filesystem erasure on session death.

A new disposable Firefox profile fixture admits an independently generated
synthetic task draft, acknowledges its authenticated replacement, confirms
**End session**, closes the tab, and terminates Firefox before inspecting
profile files. A bounded read of regular files under that profile finds no
literal UTF-8 copy of the draft. A test-owned positive-control file proves the
same scanner detects the marker when it crosses a 64 KiB read boundary; that
file is removed before the final assertion. The fixture intentionally does not
send `Clear-Site-Data`, separating the observation from browser header support.

This is a plaintext-only scan of the test profile after this orderly End
sequence, not proof against compressed, encrypted, swapped, or externally
collected copies; it does not cover drafts left by an unconfirmed close,
filesystem snapshots, a deliberate crash, or other browser engines. Session
teardown and media cleanup acceptance remain open.

A separate disposable Firefox fixture exercises the opposite branch: it
acknowledges a synthetic private task draft, verifies that the text is still
present in the live input, then sends `SIGKILL` to the browser without End,
tab close, or `pagehide`. After process exit, the same bounded profile scan
finds no literal plaintext copy. No session End request was sent; the fixture
makes no claim that the server-side session was erased. Browser crash-recovery
artifacts may still contain compressed data, and this does not simulate a
backend crash or establish deletion of all browser-managed copies.

The additional Firefox browser-history probe found that the literal initial
credential-bearing launch URL survived in the disposable `places.sqlite-wal`
file even after confirmed End or a hard browser stop. Unlike the absent task
plaintext, fragment removal alone does not erase the original navigation from
this browser's stored history. The test End fixture omits the production
`Clear-Site-Data` header, so actual runtime End erasure remains unverified.

The forced-stop case has no End header at all. Details and acceptance limits
are recorded in the open localhost security task ATR-P8-005.

A separate Linux `process_vm_readv` regression now tests kernel remote-memory
admission independently of the procfs path and open probes. Its test-only
child places a known eight-byte synthetic marker at an announced address.
The owning parent reads the exact marker when the child is dumpable, then
receives `EPERM` with an unchanged receive buffer after an otherwise identical
child invokes the production `PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0)` guard. Both children
terminate through the test-owned stdin pipe without private draft data,
crashes, core files, or persistent probe artifacts.

The same Linux fixture also tests `process_vm_writev`: a dumpable child
accepts the parent-side replacement of its marker and confirms the new bytes.
With the production guard enabled, the write returns `EPERM` and the child
confirms its original marker survived. This adds memory-integrity admission
evidence without accessing any real application session bytes.

This is direct Linux
process-memory denial evidence for the isolated guarded child, not a scan of
the real runtime's resident data, a protection against privileged inspection,
or a guarantee about swap, external browsers, or host crash collectors. P1
remains open for end-to-end temporary media and crash cleanup.

A manual Linux smoke probe also started the built Atrament CLI until `ready`
using a disposable no-op browser opener. The parent compared a normal child
process against the live runtime with the `process_vm_readv` and
`process_vm_writev` system calls, each requesting one byte at invalid remote
address zero. Both calls returned `EFAULT` for the ordinary child and `EPERM`
for Atrament; no process-private memory was read or overwritten.

The probe terminated both children and deleted its temporary opener. This
is one live-host kernel-admission observation, not an automated end-to-end
confidentiality or crash-erasure guarantee.

The prior one-off live Linux `process_vm_*` smoke is now a repeatable Cargo
integration regression. It runs the actual compiled `atrament` executable to
`ready`, using a temporary no-op `xdg-open` script under the project `.temp`
directory. The script does not save, print, or use its launch argument.

From the owning parent, both `process_vm_readv` and `process_vm_writev`
requesting one byte at invalid address zero return `EPERM`; the failed read
leaves the parent sentinel unchanged. The fixture bounds startup observation,
kills and reaps the CLI, and deletes its own opener directory.

The earlier synthetic-child fixture independently proves these syscalls are
otherwise admitted for an ordinary dumpable same-parent target.

This automated test covers compiled startup admission at `ready`, not a live
memory read, a process holding private drafts, privileged observers, crash
erasure, Firefox history cleanup, or the browser-opener argv risk. P1 stays
open for remaining session media and crash lifecycles.

A real Firefox WebDriver BiDi regression now navigates away from a populated,
fragment-scrubbed workspace to a different document and traverses browser
history back to the original tab. All three synthetic private draft fields are
empty and disabled on return. No new handshake or draft request is made even
though the test loopback server is still running. This checks actual navigation
rather than only dispatching a synthetic `pagehide` event.

A complementary generated-JavaScript VM test executes the `pageshow`
handler: a persisted-page event invokes only a reload, while an ordinary event
performs local-fragment and viewport cleanup without reloading. The real
navigation test independently confirms the intervening document has no draft
controls before requesting history traversal.

The same test uses a distinct random marker for each private draft field,
closes the browser after history traversal, and scans only regular files in
its disposable Firefox profile for literal UTF-8 bytes. All three markers
are absent; a test-owned profile control file containing each marker is
detected before removal. No explicit End request is sent, so backend
destruction is not inferred.

The fixture uses the production-like `Cache-Control: no-store` resource policy,
so the observed result does not establish which Firefox bfcache path ran or
whether other engines restore a cached page differently. It cannot prove
browser-history removal of the initial launch URL, crash erasure, or automatic
backend destruction on tab close. P1 remains open.

A separate real Firefox crash-after-navigation fixture accepts a synthetic
private task draft replacement, navigates to another document, confirms
that document has no task control, and abruptly kills Firefox without End or
tab close. A bounded literal-byte scan of its disposable profile finds no
UTF-8 copy of the random draft marker; a test-owned positive-control file
verifies that the scanner would detect the same marker before removal.

This adds page-exit-then-browser-death coverage to the existing direct hard
stop and orderly back-navigation paths. No server-side session End occurs;
the test does not claim backend destruction. Firefox recovery data may contain
compressed or encoded state, and other engines and host crash collectors are
not covered. P1 remains open for the full cleanup lifecycle.

The Firefox literal-profile regressions now cover each of the three private
`task`, `source`, and `candidate` fields, not only the task editor. Each case
posts a distinct random synthetic marker, waits for all three acknowledged
replacements and client readiness, and checks that each marker was resident
before the lifecycle transition. Confirmed End additionally requires all
three fields to be blank and disabled; both forced-death cases verify that no
End request was made. The third case first navigates to an unrelated document.

For confirmed End, direct Firefox `SIGKILL`, and `SIGKILL` after navigation,
the disposable profile scan finds none of the three literal UTF-8 markers.
Per-marker test-owned positive controls demonstrate scanner detection before
removal.

These observations do not prove erasure of encoded or compressed
copies, initial credential-bearing URL history, host crash snapshots, or
backend memory without explicit End. Other browsers and actual temporary
media files remain outside this evidence. P1 remains open.
